The attached image, Figure 4 from the "Journal of Controlled Release" paper, illustrates the uptake of Nano-11 nanoparticles and ovalbumin (OVA) antigen by bone marrow-derived dendritic cells (BMDCs) via confocal microscopy. It shows that soluble OVA alone is minimally taken up by BMDCs. In contrast, Nano-11 nanoparticles are efficiently phagocytized by DCs, and when OVA is adsorbed to Nano-11, there's a significantly increased uptake of OVA by BMDCs, with clear colocalization of the nanoparticle and the antigen inside the cells. This demonstrates Nano-11's ability to enhance antigen delivery to dendritic cells, a critical step for adjuvant function in vaccine development.